<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Myalgic encephalomyelitis/chronic fatigue syndrome, post-infectious, moderate (G93.32)</content>. She meets the NICE criteria (debilitating fatigue worsened by activity, post-exertional malaise, unrefreshing sleep, and cognitive difficulty for well over three months) and the IOM criteria, with orthostatic intolerance on the standing test (heart rate rise of 26/min without hypotension). The exclusion tests are normal apart from a low vitamin D, and the infectious diseases physician finds no evidence of relapsed or focal brucellosis. A PHQ-9 of 8 reflects the physical items of the illness, not depression. [S]<list><item>Diagnosis explained: a real, recognized illness with no diagnostic test; written care and support plan given, including a flare plan.</item><item>Energy management: keep the diary, set a baseline below the level that triggers post-exertional malaise, plan rest before and between tasks, and break work into blocks of about 30 minutes; first session with the clinic occupational therapist next week.</item><item>No graded exercise therapy and no advice to push through or exercise more.</item><item>Orthostatic intolerance: raise fluids to 2 to 2.5 L a day, add salt with meals (blood pressure 106/68 mmHg), use waist-high compression garments, and do tasks seated or reclined.</item><item>Sleep: a regular sleep window; keep daytime rests to short periods lying down.</item><item>Pain: continue acetaminophen as needed; avoid opioids and sedatives.</item><item>Work: a letter to her employer supporting home working, a reclined workstation, and flexible hours.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Personal history of treated brucellosis (Z86.19)</content>. Infectious diseases consultation today: no clinical or laboratory sign of relapse or focal infection (no fever, normal ESR and CRP, no spinal or joint findings). Repeat serology would not help. [S]<list><item>No further antibiotics or serology.</item><item>Call for fever, night sweats, or new back or joint pain.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Vitamin D deficiency (E55.9)</content>. 25-hydroxyvitamin D 17 ng/mL; she is indoors most days. [S]<list><item>Start cholecalciferol 2,000 units daily.</item><item>Recheck 25-hydroxyvitamin D in about three months.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Exclusion tests for suspected ME/CFS</td><td>Once, at diagnosis</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Drawn at intake two weeks ago and reviewed today: blood count, metabolic panel, liver tests, calcium, phosphate, ESR, CRP, TSH, HbA1c, ferritin, coeliac antibody, creatine kinase, morning cortisol, B12, and folate all normal; 25-hydroxyvitamin D low at 17 ng/mL. The intake urine dipstick was negative for protein, blood, and glucose</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Serological tests after a history of infection</td><td>By clinical judgment</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Brucella serology was positive and treated abroad; agglutinating antibodies can persist for a long time after cure, so a repeat titer would not show relapse. Infectious diseases agrees; blood cultures only if fever returns</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Ten-minute standing test for orthostatic intolerance</td><td>At diagnosis; repeat if symptoms change</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Done at intake: heart rate rise of 26/min, no orthostatic hypotension; repeat at the six-month review</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Symptom review: post-exertional malaise, sleep, pain, cognition, and orthostatic symptoms</td><td>Every review</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Diary reviewed; all four core features present</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>PHQ-9 mood screen</td><td>At diagnosis and at each review</td><td>Up to date</td><td>8 at intake, with item 9 scored 0; repeat at the three-month review</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>PROMIS fatigue and physical function T-scores, pain score</td><td>At diagnosis and at each review</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Baseline at intake: fatigue 71, physical function 32, pain 4; repeat at the three-month review</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Medication review</td><td>At diagnosis and at each review</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Acetaminophen as needed only; no sedatives or opioids. Cholecalciferol started at a usual dose</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Assessment for other conditions or persisting infection</td><td>At diagnosis if uncertain</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Infectious diseases consultation today; no evidence of active infection</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>ME/CFS specialist team to confirm the diagnosis and develop the care and support plan</td><td>At diagnosis (NICE 1.4.3)</td><td>Deferred</td><td>No ME/CFS specialist team is available in this region, a deviation from the protocol. Primary care confirmed the diagnosis by NICE and IOM criteria and wrote the care and support plan with the clinic occupational therapist; she will be offered a specialist service if one becomes available</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Energy management plan and activity and symptom diary</td><td>Every review</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Baseline set at about three hours of reclined work a day in short blocks; occupational therapist session booked</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Agree an energy management plan with a stable baseline and no more than one crash a month by the six-month review. In progress; target 2026-09-14 [S]</item><item>Keep working within her energy limits, aiming for four hours a day in planned blocks by the six-month review. In progress; target 2026-09-14 [S]</item><item>Fewer orthostatic symptoms with fluids, salt, compression, and seated or reclined tasks by the three-month review. In progress; target 2026-06-01 [S]</item><item>Replete vitamin D to 30 ng/mL or higher. In progress; target 2026-09-14 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Myalgic encephalomyelitis (or encephalopathy)/chronic fatigue syndrome: diagnosis and management (NICE guideline NG206) (2021); Diagnosing ME/CFS (2024); Grach SL, et al. Diagnosis and Management of Myalgic Encephalomyelitis/Chronic Fatigue Syndrome. Mayo Clin Proc 2023;98(10):1544-1551 (2023); Clinical Overview of Brucellosis (2024)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Occupational therapist energy management session next week. ME/CFS review in about ten weeks with PHQ-9, PROMIS scores, and 25-hydroxyvitamin D. Call for a flare that does not settle within a week, fever, or new back or joint pain. Next scheduled visit 2026-06-01: three-month ME/CFS energy management review. [S]</paragraph></text>

